A Dynamical Systems Theory of Convergence and Instability in Coupled Human–AI Networks

Author: ouadi Maakoul 

Abstract

Coupled human–artificial intelligence systems are modelled as high-dimensional, constrained dynamical systems. Under local convexity, we prove via a Razumikhin argument that the joint state converges exponentially to a unique attractor when feedback delays are small. We derive analytically a critical delay \tau_c beyond which a Hopf bifurcation destroys stability, giving rise to sustained oscillations and eventual chaos. Five sharp, falsifiable predictions are formulated concerning entropy decay, variance reduction, constraint-induced behavioural collapse, and threshold-dependent instability. A systematic corroboration against seven independently published, high-impact studies—from safe RL proofs to persona collapse experiments—demonstrates that all predictions are already empirically manifest. We provide a minimal continuous-time simulation environment and a suite of measurable metrics for direct experimental validation. The framework offers a testable mathematical foundation for designing resilient human–AI ecosystems and anticipating their failure modes.

Keywords: human–AI interaction, dynamical systems, attractors, convergence, bifurcation, delay differential equations, safe RL, persona collapse

---

1. Introduction

Human decision-makers and adaptive AI agents now form tightly coupled systems in domains from healthcare to autonomous driving. Observed phenomena range from remarkably stable collaborative routines to catastrophic feedback spirals and multi-agent collapse. Despite growing empirical evidence, no unified mathematical theory predicts when such systems stabilise and when they become dangerous.

This dissertation develops such a theory. We formalise the coupled human–AI system as a gradient-like dynamical system on a constrained manifold. Within this framework, three organising mechanisms emerge: optimisation-driven contraction, feedback-mediated stabilisation, and constraint-induced selection. Together, they imply that trajectories converge to attractor basins under broad conditions, yet remain susceptible to bifurcation when delays or noise exceed analytically derivable thresholds.

We make five specific contributions:

1. A formal state-space model with boundedly rational human and adaptive AI dynamics.
2. A local convergence theorem with explicit contraction rate and delay stability margin.
3. An analytic derivation of the critical feedback delay \tau_c for Hopf bifurcation, yielding the exact threshold for oscillatory instability.
4. A set of five falsifiable predictions and their corroboration against seven independent, high-quality studies.
5. A reproducible simulation environment and operational metrics for direct empirical testing.

The dissertation is structured as follows. Chapter 2 defines the mathematical model. Chapter 3 presents the local convergence theorem. Chapter 4 analyses instability thresholds and bifurcation. Chapter 5 states the falsifiable predictions. Chapter 6 maps those predictions onto the independent empirical literature. Chapter 7 describes the simulation environment. Chapter 8 proposes operational metrics. Chapter 9 discusses limitations and future work, and Chapter 10 concludes.

