5. Falsifiable Predictions

From the theory we extract five concrete, measurable predictions:

P1 (Exponential entropy decay). In convergent regimes (\tau < \tau_c, noise sub-threshold), the interaction entropy rate H_{\text{int}}(t) = -\sum p_t(s) \log p_t(s) decays exponentially toward a floor value H^*. Equivalently, mutual information between human and AI states increases monotonically.

P2 (Coordination variance reduction). Across repeated interactions, the variance of task performance metrics decreases monotonically as the dyad stabilises into an attractor.

P3 (Constraint-induced homogenisation). As operational constraints tighten (e.g., reduced communication bandwidth, stricter energy budgets), the number of distinct stable behavioural regimes (attractors) decreases. Multiple local basins merge into fewer, and eventually a single dominant mode survives—observable as "persona collapse" in LLM agents or convergent policies in RL.

P4 (Critical delay oscillation). There exists a computable feedback delay \tau_c. For \tau < \tau_c, performance is stable; for \tau > \tau_c, the system exhibits sustained oscillatory dynamics (e.g., belief spirals, alternating overcorrection). The onset is predicted by the leading Lyapunov exponent crossing zero.

P5 (Systemic failure due to boundary crisis). When the system trajectory hits a hard constraint (e.g., token budget exhaustion, physical safety limit), it undergoes catastrophic collapse: infinite loops, task abandonment, or state freezing. This corresponds to the attractor basin colliding with the feasible manifold boundary.

