7. Simulation Environment

We provide a minimal, reproducible simulation to enable direct testing of all predictions. The code is structured for a standard Jupyter notebook.

Model (continuous-time, asymmetrical).

\dot{h} = -\mu_H h + c_H \, a(t-\tau) + \sigma_H \epsilon_H(t),

\dot{a} = -\mu_A a + c_A \, h(t-\tau) + \sigma_A \epsilon_A(t),

with parameters \mu_H, \mu_A, c_H, c_A > 0 and Gaussian white noises.

Simulation experiments:

· Convergence regime: set \mu_H=0.5, \mu_A=0.4, c_H=0.3, c_A=0.3, \tau=0.5, noise \sigma=0.05. Observe exponential decay of distance to origin.
· Oscillatory regime: increase c_H=0.9, c_A=0.8, \tau=2.0 such that \mu_H\mu_A < c_Hc_A and \tau > \tau_c. Observe limit cycle.
· Boundary crisis: add a hard constraint |h| \le 1. With large noise or large coupling, trajectories hit the boundary and stall or oscillate violently.
· Attractor merging: simulate multiple agents with varying initial conditions under rising constraint strength (e.g., reducing feasible region). Track the final number of distinct clusters using k-means on terminal states.

