8. Measurable Metrics for Real-World Systems

To test the theory in live human–AI interactions, we propose the following operational metrics:

1. Interaction entropy rate:  H_{\text{int}} = -\frac{1}{T} \sum_{t=1}^{T} \log p(s_t | s_{<t}) , estimated from a learned transition model. Decreasing trend confirms P1.
2. Coordination cost:  \text{Var}(\text{Task Performance})  across multiple dyads over time. Reduction supports P2.
3. Attractor cardinality: number of distinct clusters of final (H, A) state pairs under identical constraints. Reducing cardinality under tighter constraints confirms P3.
4. Stability margin: maximum real part of estimated Jacobian eigenvalues from time-series embedding. A margin crossing zero indicates impending oscillation (P4).
5. Estimated feedback delay: measured via cross-correlation lag between human action and AI response, compared against the analytically computed \tau_c.
6. Constraint hit frequency: rate at which any hard constraint becomes active; a sudden spike signals an approaching boundary crisis (P5).

These metrics are compatible with existing human-subject experiment logs and LLM agent interaction traces.

