9. Discussion and Limitations

Summary of contributions. We have placed the observed regularities of human–AI interaction—stable coordination, sudden oscillations, persona collapse, catastrophic failure—within a single dynamical systems framework. The theory yields precise, testable predictions and receives strong corroboration from independent literature.

Limitations. First, the convergence theorem is local; global dynamics may involve more complex attractor landscapes. Second, the human model is still a crude reduction of bounded rationality; richer cognitive architectures could be incorporated. Third, the Hopf analysis relies on a linearised system; nonlinear effects like subcritical or global bifurcations may occur in practice. Fourth, our corroborations are retrospective; prospective controlled experiments are needed to confirm the predictions causally.

Future work. Immediate next steps include: (a) running the proposed simulation suite across a wide parameter grid; (b) collaborating with human–AI interaction labs to extract the operational metrics from existing data; (c) extending the theory to multi-agent swarms with network-delay heterogeneity; and (d) designing an online stability monitor based on the largest Lyapunov exponent.

